msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:17
msgid "`tokens` will contain the following object"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:33
msgid "Token Types"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:35
msgid "`ret.types` is a collection of the various token types exported by ret."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:37
msgid "ROOT"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:39
msgid ""
"Only used in the root of the regexp. This is needed due to the posibility"
" of the root containing a pipe `|` character. In that case, the token "
"will have an `options` key that will be an array of arrays of tokens. If "
"not, it will contain a `stack` key that is an array of tokens."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:59
msgid "GROUP"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:61
msgid ""
"Groups contain tokens that are inside of a parenthesis. If the group "
"begins with `?` followed by another character, it's a special type of "
"group. A ':' tells the group not to be remembered when `exec` is used. "
"'=' means the previous token matches only if followed by this group, and "
"'!' means the previous token matches only if NOT followed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:63
msgid ""
"Like root, it can contain an `options` key instead of `stack` if there is"
" a pipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:89
msgid "POSITION"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:91
msgid "`\\b`, `\\B`, `^`, and `$` specify positions in the regexp."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:100
msgid "SET"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:102
msgid ""
"Contains a key `set` specifying what tokens are allowed and a key `not` "
"specifying if the set should be negated. A set can contain other sets, "
"ranges, and characters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:112
msgid "RANGE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:114
msgid ""
"Used in set tokens to specify a character range. `from` and `to` are "
"character codes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:124
msgid "REPETITION"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:135
msgid "REFERENCE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:137
msgid "References a group token. `value` is 1-9."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:146
msgid "CHAR"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:148
msgid ""
"Represents a single character token. `value` is the character code. This "
"might seem a bit cluttering instead of concatenating characters together."
" But since repetition tokens only repeat the last token and not the last "
"clause like the pipe, it's simpler to do it this way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:157
msgid "Errors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:159
msgid ""
"ret.js will throw errors if given a string with an invalid regular "
"expression. All possible errors are"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:161
msgid ""
"Invalid group. When a group with an immediate `?` character is followed "
"by an invalid character. It can only be followed by `!`, `=`, or `:`. "
"Example: `/(?_abc)/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:162
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Nothing to repeat. Thrown when a repetitional token is used as the first "
"token in the current clause, as in right in the beginning of the regexp "
"or group, or right after a pipe. Example: `/foo|?bar/`, `/{1,3}foo|bar/`,"
" `/foo(+bar)/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:163
msgid "Unmatched ). A group was not opened, but was closed. Example: `/hello)2u/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:164
msgid "Unterminated group. A group was not closed. Example: `/(1(23)4/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/ret_README.md:165
msgid ""
"Unterminated character class. A custom character set was not closed. "
"Example: `/[abc/`"
msgstr ""

